---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import data from '../data/sygus.json';
import { pct } from '../lib/format';

type Method = { episodes: number; all_exact: number; exact: number[]; bits_right: number; seconds: number[] } | null;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const median = (xs: number[]) => {
  const s = [...xs].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  return s.length ? (s.length % 2 ? s[(s.length - 1) / 2]! : (s[s.length / 2 - 1]! + s[s.length / 2]!) / 2) : NaN;
};
const mean = (xs: number[]) => xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / xs.length;
const seconds = (s: number) => (s < 1 ? `${Math.round(s * 1000)} ms` : `${s < 10 ? s.toFixed(1) : Math.round(s)} s`);

// Hacker's Delight: cvc5 per file (grammar d0, d1, d5), Teenie and TabPFN per function.
const files = data.hd.files;
const solved = files.filter((f) => f.status === 'solved');
const timeouts = files.filter((f) => f.status === 'timeout');
const failed = files.filter((f) => f.status === 'failed');
const grammars = ['d0', 'd1', 'd5'];
const cvc5Cell = (fn: string, g: string) => files.find((f) => f.function === fn && f.grammar === g);
const functions = data.hd.functions;
const usable = functions.filter((f) => f.teenie);
const teenieEpisodes = usable.flatMap((f) => (f.teenie as Method)!.exact);
const teenieSeconds = usable.flatMap((f) => (f.teenie as Method)!.seconds);
const teenieAll = usable.reduce((n, f) => n + (f.teenie as Method)!.all_exact, 0);
const highFunctions = usable.filter((f) => mean((f.teenie as Method)!.exact) >= 0.98).length;
const tabpfnInfo = data.tabpfn as { version: string; setup: string; device: string } | null;
const tabpfnRan = usable.some((f) => f.tabpfn);
const tabpfnAll = usable.reduce((n, f) => n + ((f.tabpfn as Method)?.all_exact ?? 0), 0);
const tabpfnEpisodes = usable.flatMap((f) => (f.tabpfn as Method)?.exact ?? []);
const tabpfnSeconds = usable.flatMap((f) => (f.tabpfn as Method)?.seconds ?? []);

// Deep-carry set.
const problems = data.deep.problems;
const deepTimeouts = problems.filter((p) => p.cvc5 === 'timeout');
const deepExact = problems.flatMap((p) => (p.teenie as Method)?.exact ?? []);
const deepAll = problems.filter((p) => (p.teenie as Method)?.all_exact).length;
const deepTabpfn = problems.flatMap((p) => (p.tabpfn as Method)?.exact ?? []);
const profile = data.deep.bit_profile ?? [];
const fnName = (name: string) => name.replace('hd', 'hd-');
---

<Layout
  title="SyGuS bit-vector benchmarks: Teenie against cvc5"
  description="Teenie on the SyGuS-Comp Hacker's Delight bit-vector benchmarks and on a set of deep-carry rules, next to the cvc5 program synthesizer, with how each is scored."
  keywords={['SyGuS', 'program synthesis', 'cvc5', "Hacker's Delight", 'bit-vector', 'programming by example']}
  kind="article"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Results · program synthesis</p>
  <h1>Against a program synthesizer</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    SyGuS-Comp is the yearly competition for program synthesizers: given a specification, find a program that meets
    it. We took its bit-vector benchmarks and ran Teenie next to <strong>{data.solver.name}</strong>, a solver that has won
    several of its tracks. The two don't do the same job, so read the scoring first: cvc5 returns a program it has
    checked against the specification, while Teenie never writes a program. It reads 999 examples and answers new
    questions, and here it counts as right on a function only when it answers all {data.questions.toLocaleString('en-US')}
    fresh questions exactly. That is a sample, not a proof.
  </p>

  <div class="grid-3 stats">
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">cvc5 on Hacker's Delight</span>
      <span class="value">{solved.length} / {files.length} solved</span>
      <span class="note">median {seconds(median(solved.map((f) => f.seconds)))} when solved; {timeouts.length + failed.length} not solved within {data.solver.timeout} s</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">Teenie on Hacker's Delight</span>
      <span class="value">{teenieAll} / {teenieEpisodes.length} episodes</span>
      <span class="note">all {data.questions.toLocaleString('en-US')} answers exact; at least 98% exact on {highFunctions} of {usable.length} functions; median {seconds(median(teenieSeconds))} per episode</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">Deep-carry rules (ours)</span>
      <span class="value">cvc5 {problems.length - deepTimeouts.length} / {problems.length} · Teenie {deepAll} / {deepExact.length}</span>
      <span class="note">cvc5 timed out on all {deepTimeouts.length}; Teenie answered a median {pct(median(deepExact))} exactly</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>How each is scored</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <ul class="scoring">
      <li>
        <strong>cvc5</strong> ({data.solver.options}, {data.solver.timeout} s per problem, 4 at a time on a 12-core laptop
        CPU): <em>solved</em> means it returned a program. We also ran every program it returned against the reference
        function on 10,000 random inputs; none disagreed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Teenie</strong> (the release model, on an {data.teenie.device}): for each function, 5 episodes of 999
        examples and {data.questions.toLocaleString('en-US')} new questions, uniform 32-bit inputs. An episode counts
        only if every answer is exact. Its time is one pass over the examples for all the questions.
      </li>
      {
        tabpfnRan && (
          <li>
            <strong>TabPFN</strong> ({tabpfnInfo?.version}, {tabpfnInfo?.setup}, on the same GPU): the same episodes
            and questions as Teenie, scored the same way.
          </li>
        )
      }
      <li>
        Every episode was made by <code>scripts/generate-csv.ts</code>, the Try page's own formula evaluator, and every
        answer file scored by its <code>--check</code>. Each rule was translated from the benchmark's SMT-LIB into the
        Try page's formula language, and the 999 examples of every episode were checked against the original SMT-LIB
        definition.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Hacker's Delight (SyGuS-Comp General track)</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    The {functions.length} bit-twiddling functions from <em>Hacker's Delight</em> used in SyGuS-Comp 2017 to 2019, each
    with up to three grammars: d0 offers only the operators the answer needs, d1 a few more and d5 the full bit-vector
    set, so the search grows from d0 to d5. Teenie ignores grammars: it sees only examples. One function,
    {fnName('hd19')}, takes three inputs and Teenie takes two, so it has no Teenie result.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="hd">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>function</th>
          <th>as a Try-page formula</th>
          {grammars.map((g) => <th class="num">cvc5 {g}</th>)}
          <th class="num">Teenie: all exact</th>
          <th class="num">Teenie: exact</th>
          {tabpfnRan && <th class="num">TabPFN: all exact</th>}
          {tabpfnRan && <th class="num">TabPFN: exact</th>}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          functions.map((f) => {
            const t = f.teenie as Method;
            const p = f.tabpfn as Method;
            return (
              <tr>
                <td><b>{fnName(f.name)}</b></td>
                <td><code class="formula" title={f.term}>{f.formula ?? f.term}</code></td>
                {grammars.map((g) => {
                  const c = cvc5Cell(f.name, g);
                  if (!c) return <td class="num muted">·</td>;
                  return (
                    <td class={`num ${c.status === 'solved' ? 'ok' : 'bad'}`}>
                      {c.status === 'solved' ? seconds(c.seconds) : c.status}
                    </td>
                  );
                })}
                <td class="num">{t ? `${t.all_exact} / ${t.episodes}` : '·'}</td>
                <td class="num">{t ? pct(mean(t.exact)) : 'three inputs'}</td>
                {tabpfnRan && <td class="num">{p ? `${p.all_exact} / ${p.episodes}` : '·'}</td>}
                {tabpfnRan && <td class="num">{p ? pct(mean(p.exact)) : '·'}</td>}
              </tr>
            );
          })
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">
    A dot means the benchmark set has no file for that grammar.
    Teenie's <em>exact</em> is the share of the {data.questions.toLocaleString('en-US')} questions answered exactly, averaged
    over the 5 episodes.
    {tabpfnRan && ` TabPFN: ${tabpfnAll} of ${tabpfnEpisodes.length} episodes all exact, median ${seconds(median(tabpfnSeconds))} per episode.`}
  </p>
  <p>
    On these, the synthesizer is the better tool, and not only on time. With the small grammars it finds a correct program
    in milliseconds, a program that is right on every input. Teenie gets 98 to 100% of answers exact on all but one
    function, but on most it misses a few of every thousand, so it rarely gets a whole episode right. Neither does well on
    {` ${fnName('hd20')}`} (the next higher number with the same count of 1-bits, which needs a division): cvc5 solved it with
    no grammar, and Teenie answered {pct(mean((functions.find((f) => f.name === 'hd20')?.teenie as Method)?.exact ?? [0]))} exactly.
  </p>

  <h2>Deep-carry rules</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    The protocol was fixed before any result was in (it is in <code>src/teenie/bits/sygus.py</code>): {problems.length}
    random expressions in a and b from seed 0, built from +, −, xor, and, or, not, shifts by 1 to 15 and random 32-bit
    constants, with at least 4 additions or subtractions and depth 5 or more, and no multiplication or division. cvc5 got
    each as a programming-by-example problem with the same 999 examples Teenie reads, a grammar of those operators with
    any 32-bit constant, and {data.solver.timeout} s; Teenie ran on every one cvc5 timed out on.
  </p>
  <div class="grid-3 stats">
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">cvc5</span>
      <span class="value">{deepTimeouts.length} / {problems.length} timeouts</span>
      <span class="note">no program within {data.solver.timeout} s on any of them</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">Teenie, all {data.questions.toLocaleString('en-US')} exact</span>
      <span class="value">{deepAll} / {deepExact.length}</span>
      <span class="note">exact answers: median {pct(median(deepExact))}, best {pct(Math.max(...deepExact))}</span>
    </div>
    {
      tabpfnRan && (
        <div class="card stat">
          <span class="label">TabPFN, all {data.questions.toLocaleString('en-US')} exact</span>
          <span class="value">{problems.filter((p) => (p.tabpfn as Method)?.all_exact).length} / {deepTabpfn.length}</span>
          <span class="note">exact answers: median {pct(median(deepTabpfn))}</span>
        </div>
      )
    }
  </div>
  <p>
    These rules are beyond Teenie too. It gets the lowest bits right most often and does worst in the middle of the word,
    but even its lowest bit is wrong about one time in five, so this is not only a matter of long carries: rules this
    deep, with constants this large, are outside what it learned. We report the set as it came out and haven't changed
    it since seeing the results.
  </p>
  {
    profile.length > 0 && (
      <figure class="card profile">
        <div class="bars" role="img" aria-label="Share of answer bits Teenie got right on the deep-carry set, by bit position">
          {[...profile].reverse().map((v, i) => (
            <span class="bar" style={`--v:${v}`} title={`bit ${profile.length - 1 - i}: ${pct(v)} right`} />
          ))}
        </div>
        <figcaption class="small muted">
          Share of answer bits right on the deep-carry set by position, high bit on the left (the axis runs from 50%,
          a coin toss, to 100%). Lowest bit {pct(profile[0])}, worst {pct(Math.min(...profile))}.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    )
  }
  <details class="small">
    <summary>All {problems.length} rules</summary>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>rule</th><th>c =</th><th class="num">cvc5</th><th class="num">Teenie exact</th><th class="num">bits right</th>{tabpfnRan && <th class="num">TabPFN exact</th>}</tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            problems.map((p) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{p.name}</td>
                <td><code class="formula">{p.formula}</code></td>
                <td class="num">{p.cvc5}</td>
                <td class="num">{p.teenie ? pct((p.teenie as Method)!.exact[0]) : '·'}</td>
                <td class="num">{p.teenie ? pct((p.teenie as Method)!.bits_right) : '·'}</td>
                {tabpfnRan && <td class="num">{p.tabpfn ? pct((p.tabpfn as Method)!.exact[0]) : '·'}</td>}
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Paste any of them into <a href={`${base}/try/`}>Try it</a> to see it answer for yourself.</p>
  </details>

  <h2>What we didn't run</h2>
  <p>
    The SyGuS-Comp PBE bit-vector track ({data.pbe_bv.problems} problems) is the obvious other half, but {data.pbe_bv.reason},
    so there is nothing fair to run. {!tabpfnRan && 'TabPFN, the other model that predicts from examples in one pass, is not in this comparison yet.'}
  </p>

  <h2>Run it yourself</h2>
  <p class="small">
    Benchmarks from <a href={data.source.repository}>SyGuS-Org/benchmarks</a> at commit <code>{data.source.commit}</code>,
    <code>{data.source.path}</code>. Everything else is in <code>experiments/sygus/</code>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>experiments/sygus/run.sh fetch         # the benchmark files at that commit
experiments/sygus/run.sh cvc5-hd       # cvc5 on them, and its programs checked
experiments/sygus/run.sh episodes      # generate-csv.ts writes every episode
experiments/sygus/run.sh teenie-hd     # Teenie answers, generate-csv --check scores
experiments/sygus/run.sh cvc5-deep     # cvc5 on the deep-carry problems
experiments/sygus/run.sh teenie-deep
uv run python -m teenie.bits.sygus export   # this page's numbers</code></pre>
</Layout>

<style>
  .stats {
    margin: 1.2rem 0;
  }

  .scoring {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .formula {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    background: none;
  }

  table.hd td:nth-child(2) {
    max-width: 22rem;
  }

  .ok {
    color: var(--good);
  }

  .bad {
    color: var(--bad);
  }

  .profile {
    margin: 1rem 0;
  }

  .bars {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    gap: 2px;
    height: 120px;
  }

  .bar {
    flex: 1;
    height: calc((var(--v) - 0.5) * 2 * 100%);
    min-height: 2px;
    background: var(--series-1);
    border-radius: 2px 2px 0 0;
  }

  pre {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
</style>
